<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Cook is a 65-year-old man, released from prison on parole five months ago, with stage 2 hypertension and an asymptomatic stenosis of the right internal carotid artery, who comes for his Welcome to Medicare preventive examination now that his Medicare Part B has begun. Two weeks ago he started hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg, amlodipine 5 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg and aspirin 81 mg daily; he takes them from a weekly pill box and has missed no doses. He urinates more in the mornings, and has no dizziness, ankle swelling or muscle aches. His home readings this week average 142/86. He walks for an hour most days and is writing every morning. He has had no weakness, numbness, speech trouble or loss of vision. The screening tests from his first visit were reviewed with him: hepatitis C antibody nonreactive, the interferon-gamma release assay for tuberculosis negative, the HIV test nonreactive, and the hepatitis B panel negative for infection and for immunity. No vaccination records have come from the prison. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Initial preventive physical examination, age 65</td></tr><tr><td>New medicines</td><td>Hydrochlorothiazide, amlodipine, atorvastatin and aspirin for two weeks; no missed doses</td></tr><tr><td>Home blood pressure</td><td>Average 142/86 this week</td></tr><tr><td>Stroke symptoms</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Screening results</td><td>Hepatitis C, tuberculosis and HIV negative; hepatitis B not immune</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>PHQ-2 1</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on paper in the waiting room and reviewed with him face to face by Dr. Okafor-Brandt. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Fair; better since the room is settled</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Few friends nearby; reports to a parole officer monthly; feels ashamed when recognized</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Walks about an hour most days</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Cooks simply on a hot plate; trying to eat less salt and more vegetables</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Six to seven hours</td></tr><tr><td>Oral health</td><td>No dental care for years; two loose back teeth, no pain</td></tr><tr><td>Safety</td><td>Smoke alarm in the rooming house; no firearms</td></tr><tr><td>Falls</td><td>No falls in the past year; steady on stairs</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent in all, including medicines and money</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and vision</td><td>Reading glasses; hearing normal by his report</td></tr><tr><td>Cognition</td><td>No concerns by his report</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

